Two fixes Humid needs to run the allowance covenant end to end

Both bugs are filed upstream and unfixed as of 2026-10-07. Apply this from the root of
a Humid checkout and rebuild:

    git apply /path/to/humid-allowance-fixes.patch
    bun run build        # then reload the extension at chrome://extensions

Applies to:  https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/humid
Verified against both `7dc8535` ("Dev (#19)") and `cb1b71c` ("release: 1.1.2"), which
are byte-identical in all five files touched here.

Check whether either fix has landed upstream before applying. If both have, you do not
need this file. The quickest test: grep for the two refusal strings quoted below. If
`git apply` rejects this against a newer Humid, the two changes are described well enough
below to redo by hand.

If it fails with "packages/tx-manifest/src/evaluation/inputRules.test.ts: already exists
in working directory", you already have that file from a previous run of this patch, and
`git stash` does not stash untracked files. Delete it and apply again, or skip it with
`git apply --exclude='*inputRules.test.ts'` (it only adds tests for the new function; the
fixes work without it).

--- 1. Relative timelocks were refused outright -------------------------------

Symptom: every PartialWithdraw and FullWithdraw fails with

    Input allowance_in asks for a relative timelock, and this wallet sets one
    sequence for the whole transaction — which would time-lock the outputs
    funding it as well, against their own age rather than this input's.
    { "reject": "unimplemented-construct", "reason": "invalid_manifest_request" }

The stated rationale is real in general: one shared nSequence would wrongly time-lock
a separate fee-paying input against its own confirmation age, and the underlying smplx
wasm builder genuinely offers no per-input sequence, but `transactionSequence()` fired
whenever any input declared a relative timelock, without inspecting how many inputs the
transaction actually has. This contract permits exactly one
(`enforce_single_input()`), so the refusal never applied to it.

Fix: `relativeTimelockPlacement()` in `evaluation/inputRules.ts` refuses only when some
*other* input would really inherit the wait, and `reviewManifestAction()` calls it after
`placeInputs()`, where the real placement is known.

--- 2. An action whose only output is change was refused as "pays nothing" ----

Symptom: FullWithdraw fails on an instance with real balance remaining, with

    "FullWithdraw" pays nothing, so there is nothing to build.

A policy-asset "change" output is deliberately left out of `review.outputs`, because
the builder's own `addChange()` computes its value instead. FullWithdraw's only output
is such an output, so `review.outputs` came out empty and
`assembleReviewedTransaction()` refused before `addChange()`, which would have paid
the beneficiary correctly, ever ran. PartialWithdraw is unaffected only because it has
a second, explicit output that keeps the list non-empty.

Fix: the review records `changeDeferred` when it leaves a policy-asset change output
for the builder, and the refusal now fires only when the outputs are empty *and* no
change was deferred. The optional field keeps every existing ManifestReview literal,
tests included, type-checking unchanged. Deliberately not fixed by computing a real
surplus in the review: that would risk both the review and `addChange()` adding an
output for the same value.

Test changes are included so `bun test` stays green: two existing tests asserted the
old unconditional timelock refusal.

Both fixes verified end to end against a patched build on 2026-10-07: Fund,
PartialWithdraw and FullWithdraw all built, signed and broadcast on Liquid testnet.

